00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.530 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  this is a red abalone it's basically the  oceans version of a garden snail  it lives primarily on large rocks in the  lush kelp forests of California it has  also been a popular delicacy in the  state for over a century while wild red  abalone is not available to commercial  fishermen recreational abalone divers in  Northern California can snorkel down to  the rocks pry off a snail and bring it  home for a barbecue that is until  recently something is happening to the  kelp forests and it's threatening the  survival of the species  and the Northern California communities  that rely on the red abalone for  business in December of 2018 California  Fish and Game decided to close down the  recreational abalone fishery until 2021  in hopes that they might give this sea  snail population a chance to Reba my  name is Jo Cresselia I've been diving  since 1969 50 years the North California  coast is just an incredibly beautiful  place the typography the really high  cliffs and big rocks and stick out on  the water it's almost prehistoric in  that way you feel like you've really  gone off into another world  [Music]  we're catching up here or the red  abalone there's a variety of abalone but  red abalone traditionally is what your  grandparents would think of and they  were seeing abalone on the coast  for people they think they don't like  fish for people that don't think they  like selfish it's amazing to see their  eyes just Farkle and they take a bite a  look oh this is absolutely incredible  and you know before they took the bite  they were almost afraid to take a bite  in 1916 California established a  commercial fishery for wild red abalone  but in 1997 commercial fishery closed  due to a dwindling population twenty  years later at the recreational fishery  closed when purple sea urchins ravaged  the kelp forests and left the red  abalone dying of starvation  I appreciate what Fish &amp; Game has done  and I think anybody intelligently does  and it's recently closing it down was  something I had predicted by watching  the ocean there was a couple places I'd  literally been diving for 50 years and  there really wasn't enough kelp you can  see it you would see the kelp stumps and  four or five sea urchins on it just  chomping it looked like someone had come  in with a chainsaw and just cut it  you're going down you're seeing like a  blanket of sea urchins the cult was  disappearing the abalone you weren't  seeing as many of because they were off  looking for food and there was an  unbalanced I were a dive watch that  gives me my depth and my water  temperature and I was noticing the water  temperature getting higher and I was  used to the water being 49:51 kind of in  that range sometimes she'd be in a  little cold it would be a little warmer  but I was seeing spikes where the water  was 54 or 55 degrees at times for 40  years and seen pretty much the same type  of temperature consistently and to see  those type of variances made me wonder  the bottoms changing there's no question  about it  and the and you can see olds abalone  shells broken stuff for everyone and  died  [Music]  so yeah  whose life's changed on the bottom now  it's going through going through a  change my name is dr. Laura Rogers  Bennett and I am a senior environmental  scientist here at the bodega Marine  Laboratory abalone are very much an  indicator of the health of the kelp  forests they are very sensitive to any  changes in food abundance they're not  able to switch over and eat meat and so  when their numbers go down we know  there's something going wrong in the  kelp forest  in 2013 we had a sea star wasting  disease that hit our coasts really hard  and that took out a lot of predatory sea  stars after that we had the warm blob  the El Nino of 2014 and 15 up in the  Gulf of Alaska there formed a warm water  blob and the oceanographers called it a  blob and so that warm water sat in a  patch and extended all the way down to  our region and that feature set up and  didn't move and just stayed there not  took out all of our kilt because the  water got too warm for our kelp to  survive studies done on the blob show  that while there were a number of  natural drivers that formed it  human-generated global warming likely  made it worse shortly after that we had  the purple urchin explosion of around  2015 and 16 they can mow down forests  like lawn mowers and they multiply very  quickly and they grow very quickly they  grow a lot quicker than species like  abalone so the abalone can't really keep  up with their eating habits the purple  urchin are taking the kelp before it can  even get to a mature age so they're  eating baby kelps before they can  reproduce and that's what's causing this  huge urgent barren that we've been  seeing  [Music]  usually in a healthy ecosystem a red  abalone would be hidden under a rock  lately we've been seeing them on top of  rocks and they're almost half to  three-quarters of their body are perched  up and they're just waiting for anything  to drift by and float into their muscle  or their mouth and so it's a little bit  of an odd sighting some divers have a  little bit of kelp they keep in their  pockets in case they pass a red abalone  and you can put kelp in front of an  abalone and it will lurch it's really  wild to watch it will actually lurch up  like a puppy dog that just smelled a  treat and it will almost unhinge its  whole body just to go grab that piece of  kelp and it tucks it in and it kind of  like holds it from all the other  merchants that are usually around it  we collected new numbers as of 2018 and  we don't see any improvement in the  abalone population if anything we saw  more declines  so we typically see around 24 to 26  thousand divers coming to dive and rock  pick and collect abalone but they're  also coming with a lot of their friends  and family it's estimated that the  recreational red abalone fishery is  worth about 44 million dollars to the  local communities and to the fishermen  who are participating in the fishery  Silver's at the wharf is a full-service  seafood and steak restaurant and 17  lodging rooms which are used principally  by fishermen my name is Jim Hurst and we  are in front of Silver's at the wars and  Noyo Harbor Fort Bragg California the  impact of the abalone closure is  somewhere about 30,000 to 40,000 dollars  a month that's attributed to lots of  food sales and lodging sales once you  lose that business you don't get it back  it's it's interesting to compare what's  going on today to when our mill closed  and when the mill closed everybody fat  oh my God we're gonna die that's it's  gonna have a huge impact it can't be  replaced my name is Chris Bryan's we're  here at harvest market I am a grocery  clerk I have worked here for 29 years  there is a large amount of people that  came to Fort Bragg to go diving it the  whole family would come April first  comes and goes now whereas before you  could see the influx of the people that  are going diving I think seeing and  understanding how interconnected  everything is being able to pick your  food out of the ocean like that you have  an appreciation for the effort that goes  into it and where it comes from  this isn't from some abstract store  someplace this is actually your  activities and you see how it grows and  there's a relationship it should be a  relationship of stewardship this is a  source of food and you want to protect  it  and you want to take care of it so that  you can continue to do it it's a  renewable resource as long as it's  properly handed as the globe warms we're  going to have more marine heat waves  we're going to have longer duration heat  waves and they're going to be broader  geographic extent and so I think this is  really giving us an inkling of what is  to come it's quite sad that these people  that have done this for generations have  to stop  [Music]  it's interesting recently I was talking  to some people and somebody made a  comment yeah nature's gonna heal itself  question is will man go along with it or  not or will he make the environment so  intolerable for itself that that'll be  it  I'd like the mankind will go on also and  be part of this  [Music]  [Music]

